SUMMARY:[現場] Who’s Afraid of the East Romans?
LOCATION:Encina Commons\, Room 123
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/whos-afraid-of-the-east-romans
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/whos-afraid-of-the-east-roman
 s\n\nRecent years have seen a push to recognize the east Roman state and i
 ts people for what and who they said they were — Romans — and not to h
 ide that reality under the invented term “Byzantium\,” which has alway
 s encoded negative stereotypes. In this talk\, professor Kaldellis will di
 scuss the ideologies and structural interests that have resisted acknowled
 ging a Roman people in the east\, ranging from western Eurocentrism to mod
 ern Greek nationalism. “Rome" has long been a foundational matrix for th
 inking about western identities and states: what would it look like if it 
 made room to accommodate its eastern branch on an equal basis?\n\nRSVP her
 e.\n\nAnthony Kaldellis is a professor of Classics at the University of Ch
 icago. He has published widely on the history\, culture\, and literature o
 f Byzantium\, ranging from the fourth to the fifteenth centuries. His work
 s include a new\, comprehensive history of the eastern Roman empire: The N
 ew Roman Empire: A History of Byzantium (Oxford\, 2023). Most recently he 
 has published a new history of the fall of Constantinople in 1453 and a st
 udy of western perceptions of Byzantium from late antiquity to World War I
 I (Phantom Byzantium). He has translated medieval Greek authors\, includin
 g historians such as Prokopios\, Attaleiates\, and Laonikos Chalkokondyles
 \, and is the host of the academic podcast “Byzantium & Friends\,” goi
 ng strong since 2019.
